==================================================
WHY THIS IS A WEBSITE, NOT AN EXTENSION
==================================================

The frontend is only the user interface for creating and managing watches.

The actual product is the backend process that runs while the user’s browser is closed.

A browser extension is inappropriate because:

- Browsers may be closed.
- Extension background workers may be suspended.
- Email-provider secrets cannot safely live in client code.
- Every user would make separate requests to Waterloo.
- It would unnecessarily restrict the service to certain browsers.

The server should fetch each distinct course only once per polling cycle, even if many people watch sections in that course.

For example, if 300 users watch sections in CS 246, perform one request for that course, parse all sections, then evaluate all 300 watches locally.

==================================================
SCHEDULING REQUIREMENTS
==================================================

Waterloo states that its Schedule of Classes is updated every 30 minutes between 8:00 a.m. and 8:00 p.m.

The exact completion time of each update is not yet proven.

For the initial MVP, schedule checks at:

- 8:01 a.m.
- 8:31 a.m.
- 9:01 a.m.
- 9:31 a.m.
- Continuing every 30 minutes
- Final check at 8:01 p.m.

Use the timezone:

America/Toronto

Do not use a fixed UTC offset because daylight-saving time changes.

Make the polling minutes configurable through environment variables so we can later change from :01/:31 to :03/:33 without editing application code.

Also log:

- When each polling cycle starts
- Which distinct courses were fetched
- Whether the request succeeded
- Whether data changed
- How many watches were evaluated
- Whether notifications were queued or sent
- Any errors

Do not let one failed course request crash the entire polling cycle.

For the MVP, APScheduler may run inside the FastAPI process, but document this restriction:

- The deployment must run exactly one scheduler instance.
- Running multiple web instances would cause duplicate jobs.
- A future production version should use a separate worker, database lock, or distributed job system.

Do not introduce those larger systems now.

==================================================
CORE NOTIFICATION LOGIC
==================================================

The system must notify only when a section transitions from full to potentially open.

Example:

Previous state:
capacity = 70
enrollment = 70
available = 0

Current state:
capacity = 70
enrollment = 69
available = 1

That should trigger an email.

Use logic equivalent to:

was_full = old_enrollment_total >= old_enrollment_capacity
is_now_open = new_enrollment_total < new_enrollment_capacity

if was_full and is_now_open:
    notify_watchers()

Important edge cases:

1. First observation

If there is no saved previous state, save the current state but do not send an opening alert.

Otherwise, every section that is already open when the application launches would generate a misleading notification.

2. Section remains open

Do not send an email every polling cycle while the section remains open.

3. Section fills and opens again

This sequence may send two notifications:

full -> open -> full -> open

4. Duplicate polling execution

The polling and notification logic should be idempotent enough that an accidental repeated execution does not immediately send duplicate emails.

Use a notification record or a clearly designed state check if needed. Explain the chosen approach.

5. Capacity changes

Consider what should happen if Waterloo changes a section’s capacity rather than enrollment total.

A transition from full to open because capacity increased should still count as a potential opening.

6. Waterloo errors

Do not treat missing, malformed, or failed data as zero enrollment or an open section.

